Therefore, the solution is to update the tabbable nodes after removing an item in the basket, so that the focus trap has an accurate set of tabbable nodes.

We can do this by calling updateTabbableNodes on the focus trap after we remove the item and update the count.

But note: we don't have direct access to the focus trap instance in the click handler.

However, we can store the focus trap instance in a global variable or retrieve it from the backdrop element? Alternatively, we can modify the focus trap to expose a method to update tabbable nodes, but we are not allowed to change the focus trap code? We are only allowed to change the given files: index.html, index.js, and upstream-dependency/tabbable/index.js.

But note: the focus trap is created in index.js. We can store it in a variable that is accessible in the click handler.

Let's look at index.js:

  const backdrop = document.getElementById('backdrop');
  const trap = createFocusTrap('#basket', { onDeactivate: () => { backdrop.hidden = true; } });
  document.getElementById('open').addEventListener('click', () => { backdrop.hidden = false; trap.activate(); });
  document.getElementById('close').addEventListener('click', () => trap.deactivate());
  document.getElementById('items').addEventListener('click', event => {
    const button = event.target.closest('button');
    if (!button) return;
    button.closest('li').remove();
    const left = document.querySelectorAll('#items li').length;
    document.getElementById('count').textContent = left + (left === 1 ? ' item' : ' items');
