We can modify the click handler to also update the trap's tabbable nodes after removal.

But note: the focus trap does not have a public method to update tabbable nodes. It has updateContainerElements, but that is for changing the container elements. We don't want to change the container elements, we just want to update the tabbable nodes within the existing container.

Looking at the focus trap code, we see that there is no public method to update tabbable nodes without changing the containers. However, we can call the private updateTabbableNodes function? But it's not exposed.

Alternatively, we can note that the focus trap's updateContainerElements method, when called with the same elements, will update the tabbable nodes. Because inside updateContainerElements:

  updateContainerElements(containerElements) {
    const elementsAsArray = [].concat(containerElements).filter(Boolean);

    state.containers = elementsAsArray.map((element) =>
      typeof element === 'string' ? doc.querySelector(element) : element
    );

    if (config.isolateSubtrees) {
      collectAdjacentElements(state.containers);
    }

    if (state.active) {
      updateTabbableNodes();   // <--- this is what we want
    }

    updateObservedNodes();

    return this;
  }

So if we call trap.updateContainerElements('#basket') (or the same element we passed initially), it will update the tabbable nodes.

But note: we are passing a selector string. We can also pass the element.

We have the basket element: we can get it by document.getElementById('basket').

